<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Farouk of Egypt</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1920">1920</a>–<a href="/years/1965">1965</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as King Farouk</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Farouk was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> from 1936 to 1952, a member of the Muhammad Ali dynasty. His reign was marked by British interference, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdeen-palace">Abdeen Palace</a> incident of 1942, defeat in the 1948 Arab–Israeli War and growing public anger at corruption. The Free Officers forced him to abdicate in July 1952, and he died in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/farouk-of-egypt.jpg" alt="Farouk of Egypt" width="320" height="419" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Farouk-King-.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Farouk was born at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdeen-palace">Abdeen Palace</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> on 11 February 1920, the eldest child of Sultan Fuad I, later King Fuad I, and his second wife Nazli Sabri. His family had Albanian, Circassian, Turkish, French, Greek and Egyptian ancestry. Fuad I died on 28 April 1936, and the sixteen-year-old Farouk left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> to return as king, reaching <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> on 6 May 1936, where large crowds greeted him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>A regency council governed until 29 June 1937, when Farouk turned 17 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamic-calendar">Islamic lunar calendar</a> and was counted as 18. He was crowned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> on 29 July 1937. He cultivated an image as a pious king and courted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muslim-brotherhood">Muslim Brotherhood</a> as a counterweight to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wafd-party">Wafd Party</a> of Nahas Pasha, whose government he dismissed in the autumn of 1937. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> stayed neutral in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, though under British pressure he broke relations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in September 1939.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> ended the war as a creditor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, yet wealth was very unevenly spread: the kingdom had 500 millionaires while most peasants lived in great poverty, and a cholera epidemic in 1947 killed 80,000 people. Farouk's popularity declined. British troops left the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> valley in 1946 but kept their base on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suez-canal">Suez Canal</a>. In May 1948 Farouk overruled Prime Minister Mahmoud El Nokrashy Pasha, who had warned that the army was not ready, and took Egypt into war with Israel, fearing that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muslim-brotherhood">Muslim Brotherhood</a> would otherwise gain strength.</p><p>On 17 October 1951 Parliament cancelled the Anglo-Egyptian Treaty of 1936, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> attacks on the Suez base followed. On 25 January 1952 British forces besieged the police station at Ismailia, and 43 policemen and 3 soldiers were killed. The next day, 26 January 1952, known as Black Saturday, rioters burned much of downtown <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>; 26 people died. Farouk dismissed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naha">Nahas</a> and then changed prime ministers repeatedly over the following six months.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Farouk was overthrown in July 1952 and forced to abdicate in favour of his six-month-old son Ahmed Fuad, who reigned formally as Fuad II until the monarchy was abolished in June 1953. Farouk settled first in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> and then in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. In October 1952 he was indicted for treason in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, though no extradition request was made. He died in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> on 18 March 1965. His wealth and lavish way of life made him known abroad as a playboy king, and the corruption of his government was widely condemned in Egypt.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Farouk of Egypt” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>King Farouk</b> abdicated in favour of his infant son Fuad II, but authority lay with the Revolutionary Command Council, which banned all political parties.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-egypt">Republic of Egypt · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>King Farouk</b> restored the royal anthem in 1936, and after the end of the monarchy it was kept under a new name, the Egyptian Republican anthem.</blockquote><a href="/songs/biladi-biladi-biladi">Biladi, Biladi, Biladi</a></li><li><blockquote>Army officers led by Muhammad Naguib and Gamal Abdel Nasser seized power in a bloodless coup, forcing <b>King Farouk</b> to abdicate days later.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-23">23 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1942 British tanks surrounded Abdeen Palace to force <b>Farouk</b> to appoint a Wafd government, and the Axis advance was halted at El Alamein.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-egypt">Kingdom of Egypt</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdeen-palace">Abdeen Palace</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=30.04,31.25&amp;year=1949" aria-label="Abdeen Palace on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Farouk of Egypt"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5092 4996h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5092 4996h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=30.04,31.25&amp;year=1949">What was here in 1949? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
